.TH BURNBAG-VIEWER 1 "September 2026" "burnbag 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
burnbag-viewer \- browse burnbag power history in a GTK 4 desktop application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B burnbag-viewer
.RI [ \-\-last " DURATION" | \-\-from " TIME" \-\-to " TIME" ]
.RI [ \-\-only ]
.RI [ \-\-automation " SOCKET_PATH" ]
.br
.B burnbag-viewerctl
.RI \-\-socket " SOCKET_PATH COMMAND"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B burnbag-viewer
is a read-only GTK 4 application for burnbag's system and per-user telemetry
history. With no range options, it covers all history available in both the
system and user databases. The graph summarizes all numeric samples in a
bounded, extrema-preserving overview. The measurement table loads ordered
history in pages and exposes local date and time first, followed by record
attributes and measurement columns discovered across the complete history.
Search scans both available history databases without blocking the UI.
.PP
The Fields... button opens Graph and Table pages with two columns of named
checkboxes. Choose graph measurements and table columns independently.
OK saves and applies both selections; Cancel, Escape and closing the dialog
discard all changes. Enter activates OK. Neither view changes while editing.
Date and Time always remain the first table columns. Empty selections are valid.
All selected traces overlay the exact same plot rectangle. Measurements with the
same units share one Y range containing every displayed value for that unit;
different units autorange independently. Axis strips alternate from the outside
inward: first unit left, second right, third inward on the left, and so on.
Unit titles are centered along their strips and rotated 90 degrees counter-clockwise.
Numeric ticks aim for ten divisions, reduced to keep at least 1.5 label heights
between labels. Unknown numeric fields receive individual named units; device
brightness counts share a scale only within the same device.
.PP
The color key sits inside the lower center of the plot, with a 50%-alpha background
box and opaque labels colored to match their traces. Labels wrap inside the box;
enlarge the window or select fewer fields if axes or the key cannot fit.
.PP
Left-drag highlights an inclusive time interval without panning. Reverse drags
work; endpoints clamp to the plot edges and small click jitter is ignored.
Use arrow buttons or keys to pan without changing the selection.
Clicks in axis strips do not
select observations; clicks inside the plot choose the nearest displayed point
across all traces in pixel space. A graph click sets the cursor; double-click
also clears the highlighted interval and stays on Graph.
On Table, only rows in the highlighted interval are navigable, or all allowed
history if there is no highlight. Search intersects the interval. The cursor row
is highlighted by record ID when included; loading it never hides earlier rows.
.PP
Fit sits between the minus and plus zoom buttons and is disabled without a
highlight. It fits the graph exactly to the highlighted interval, retaining the
table filter and cursor. Plus halves and minus doubles the current graph time
span about its midpoint, then sets the highlight and table interval to match.
The mouse wheel uses finer synchronized zoom increments. Zoom has a one-second
minimum span; hard range locks still apply. Y axes continue to autorange.
A table row double-click opens the graph around that record. Select table rows
and choose View selection to highlight and fit their interval on Graph.
Right-click resets the initial graph viewport and clears cursor/highlight;
All history also clears search and restores all allowed data in both views.
F11 toggles fullscreen; in graph fullscreen, only the graph is shown and it
fills the application window. Native GNOME window controls remain available in
normal mode.
.PP
History is opened in SQLite read-only mode. When both databases are available,
records are merged by timestamp and ID; system records take precedence on ID
collisions and system coverage suppresses overlapping user measurements.
Absent or unreadable sources are reported in the window. No database is created
or modified.
.PP
The full-history graph is downsampled by time bucket while retaining each
bucket's first, last, minimum and maximum for each numeric measurement.
Continuity is determined from the original observations, not the spacing
between reduced points; isolated observations remain visible. Zooming reloads
detail for the current viewport. The table itself
remains paged; the window reports the total merged row count and loads further
rows as the user scrolls.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-last " DURATION"
Set the initial graph viewport from DURATION ago to one snapshot
of the current time. For example, 5h, '5 H', 'five hours', 5:00:00 and 05:00:00
all mean five hours; 5:00 means five minutes. Quote parameters containing spaces.
Seconds, minutes, hours, days, weeks, months, years, decades, centuries and
millennia are supported, including compound durations such as '1h 30m'.
Units are case insensitive; m means minutes and mo means months.
Days and weeks are fixed elapsed time (24 hours per day).
Months and larger units use calendar subtraction at the same local date/time,
clamping the day to the earlier month's last day. Calendar components must
total whole months. Ambiguous/nonexistent local times and unsupported calendar
years are rejected. Cannot be combined with --from or --to.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " TIME"
Set the initial start using ISO 8601, with an explicit UTC offset or Z when
needed. Without --to, the end is now. Local times must be unambiguous.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " TIME"
Set the initial end using ISO 8601. Without --from, the start is 24 hours earlier.
.TP
.B \-\-only
Require a range supplied by --last or --from/--to and restrict both databases,
table/search, graph and navigation to that inclusive fixed interval. Zooming
and panning within the interval remain available; all controls and automation
clamp at its boundaries. Without --only, the range sets only the initial view
and navigation may leave it. With no range options, show all available history.
.PP
All history expands the graph and table to the complete allowed history;
with --only this button is named All in range and retains the boundary.
A right-button graph reset restores the initial viewport. Search spans all
allowed records without resetting the graph viewport.
.TP
.BI \-\-automation " SOCKET_PATH"
Enable the opt-in, same-user Unix-domain control socket at the absolute path
.I SOCKET_PATH.
The parent directory must be owned by the current user and not writable by
group or others; an existing socket path is never replaced. The socket is
removed on application shutdown only if it is still the socket created by this
process. Normal launches do not create an automation endpoint.
.TP
.BR \-h , \-\-help
Show command-line help and exit.
.SH AUTOMATION
Use
.B burnbag-viewerctl --socket SOCKET_PATH
with one of the commands
.BR status ,
.BR capture ,
.BR key ,
.BR type ,
.BR tab ,
.BR row ,
.BR select ,
.BR series ,
.BR fields ,
.BR view ,
.BR fit ,
.BR click ,
.BR pointer ,
.BR zoom ,
.BR pan , or
.BR window .
The newline-delimited JSON protocol bounds requests, correlates responses by
request ID, and accepts connections only from the viewer's effective user ID.
It supports semantic keyboard input, pointer movement/buttons/wheel, row and
range selection, tab changes, navigation, window state and capture. The capture
command returns PNG bytes for the complete GTK client area. It does not include
GNOME's server-side titlebar and decorations, which belong to the window
manager. Keep the socket inside a private per-user runtime directory such as
.BR "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" .
.PP
Use fields open, fields set --view graph|table --name FIELD --checked yes|no,
fields tab --view graph|table, fields ok, and fields cancel to operate the dialog.
Status includes applied choices, available fields and draft selections/errors.
The graph_layout state includes the shared plot rectangle, ordered unit axes,
their ranges, ticks and strips, trace colors and the half-alpha legend geometry.
State also exposes selected_range, cursor (record ID and timestamp), fit_enabled,
table_bounds, table_loading, table_has_more and cursor_row_selected. The fit
command and pointer target use the same handler as the toolbar button.
Capture returns the dialog client frame while it is open. Return, Escape and
window close act on the dialog; background navigation is blocked.
The legacy series NAME command selects a single field for this run; Fields OK
persists choices.
.SH PREFERENCES
Graph and table selections are saved together in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/burnbag/viewer.json
(default
.IR $HOME/.config/burnbag/viewer.json ).
An unset, empty or relative XDG_CONFIG_HOME uses the default.
OK publishes the private file atomically. A failed save leaves the dialog open
with an error and does not change either view. Invalid or unreadable preferences
produce a visible warning and use defaults until a successful OK.
Defaults are the first percentage measurement (otherwise first numeric field)
and all table columns. Saved empty selections stay empty. Fields absent from the
current history retain their saved choices; newly discovered fields start
unchecked after an explicit selection. Concurrent viewers use the last saved
selection. Field changes preserve time range, search, rows and table selection.
.SH DATA
The system database is
.I /var/lib/burnbag/history.sqlite3
and per-user history is stored beneath
.IR "$XDG_STATE_HOME/burnbag/history.sqlite3"
(or
.IR "$HOME/.local/state/burnbag/history.sqlite3" ).
The viewer never writes to either path. Data availability follows the invoking
user's read permissions. The status line shows per-source counts or missing/error
states; its tooltip gives the actual database paths and errors. Each launch
reads a bounded snapshot of the stores so a running collector cannot make a
query grow indefinitely; reopen to see subsequently recorded observations.
Live data refresh is backlog-only, not implemented in this version.
The older burnbag.log operational JSON-lines file is separate and is not a
SQLite telemetry source.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
GTK 4.6 or later, PyGObject with Cairo integration for the system Python,
and a running graphical session are
required. The viewer is separate from the terminal-only
.B burnbag
command.
.PP
The current automation PNG capture implementation additionally needs
PyGObject 3.48 or later for GTK render-node support. Older bindings can display
the viewer but return a capture error; installation does not currently check
this optional capability.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR burnbag (1)
